---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="About" description="Learn about CheckYourCalendar, its mission to bridge utility and cultural storytelling, and the science behind calendar conversions.">
  <Header activeTab="about" />

  <main class="container main-content">
    <article class="editorial-article">
      <header class="article-header">
        <h1 class="article-title">About CheckYourCalendar</h1>
        <p class="article-subtitle">Bridging mathematical coordinates and cultural stories</p>
      </header>

      <section class="article-body">
        <p class="lead">
          Time is not merely a number. Every day has a context. Every date has a history. Yet, timekeeping sites are traditionally split between dry utility grids and hyper-siloed religious portals. CheckYourCalendar exists to bridge that gap.
        </p>

        <h2>Our Mission: The Date Discovery Path</h2>
        <p>
          We operate under a simple, progressive information architecture:
        </p>
        <div class="mission-path">
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">1</span>
            <strong>Date</strong>
            <p>A specific coordinate in time</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-arrow"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_forward</span></div>
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">2</span>
            <strong>Calendar</strong>
            <p>How different cultures map that date</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-arrow"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_forward</span></div>
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">3</span>
            <strong>Observance</strong>
            <p>What festivals or fasts occur today</p>
          </div>
          <div class="path-arrow"><span class="material-symbols-outlined">arrow_forward</span></div>
          <div class="path-step">
            <span class="step-num">4</span>
            <strong>Meaning & Context</strong>
            <p>The cultural and historical significance behind it</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          CheckYourCalendar is designed to let you check a conversion instantly, but walk away with a deeper understanding of what today actually represents across human traditions.
        </p>

        <h2 id="methodology">Calculation Methodology</h2>
        <p>
          Calendar calculations are complex and high-trust. We utilize standard, open-source astronomical and mathematical adapters to compute dates programmatically:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Solar Calendars</strong> (e.g., Gregorian, Persian) use precise solar cycle calculations, mapping astronomical coordinates or leap rules directly.</li>
          <li><strong>Lunisolar Calendars</strong> (e.g., Hebrew, Chinese) balance the lunar cycles with solar correction. We use official astronomical models to calculate leap additions and seasonal adjustments.</li>
          <li><strong>Pure Lunar Calendars</strong> (e.g., Hijri) follow the moon sighting cycle. For calculation predictability, we utilize the Umm al-Qura calendar civil calculations as a standard, while clearly documenting that visual sighting variances may differ by 1 day regionally.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Research & Verification Standards</h2>
        <p>
          We hold cultural and religious content to high accuracy standards:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No Mass AI Filler:</strong> All descriptions and historical contexts are curated and fact-checked. We do not mass-generate spiritual claims or legends.</li>
          <li><strong>Attribution:</strong> We reference academic publications, astronomical ephemerides, government gazettes, and established cultural/religious archives.</li>
          <li><strong>Neutral Tone:</strong> We present cultural and religious traditions informatively and respectfully, without promotion or sectarian bias.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Which calendars are supported in V1?</h3>
            <p>
              CheckYourCalendar currently provides full mathematical and cultural conversion for 8 major systems: <strong>Vikram Samvat (Gujarati/Hindu)</strong>, <strong>Hijri (Islamic Civil / Umm al-Qura)</strong>, <strong>Hebrew</strong>, <strong>Persian (Solar Hijri)</strong>, <strong>Indian National (Saka)</strong>, <strong>Chinese Traditional (Lunisolar)</strong>, <strong>Coptic (Alexandrian)</strong>, and <strong>Ethiopian (Ge'ez)</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why might a traditional date vary by one day regionally?</h3>
            <p>
              Certain lunar and lunisolar traditions (such as Islamic Hijri and Hindu Panchang) historically rely on local astronomical conditions—such as physical crescent moon sightings or sunrise tithi timings in specific timezones. We use standardized civil and astronomical algorithms as a baseline, while noting regional variance factors on relevant observance pages.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is the difference between a calendar system and an observance?</h3>
            <p>
              A <strong>calendar system</strong> is a mathematical and astronomical framework for dividing time (years, months, cycles). An <strong>observance</strong> is a specific cultural festival, religious holy day, fast, or commemoration that occurs on designated coordinates within that system.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>How do I report a discrepancy or suggest an observance?</h3>
            <p>
              If you notice a calendar calculation variance or wish to suggest an observance for addition, please reach out via our <a href="/contact">Contact Page</a>. We review every submission against primary astronomical and cultural sources.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .main-content {
    padding-top: var(--spacing-xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .editorial-article {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .article-header {
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-lg);
  }

  .article-title {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    line-height: 1.15;
  }

  .article-subtitle {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-variant);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .article-body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-on-background);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.625rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-md);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-outline-variant);
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .article-body p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-on-background);
  }

  .article-body ul {
    list-style-type: square;
    padding-left: var(--spacing-lg);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-on-background);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  /* FAQ Section Styling */
  .faq-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .faq-item {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-outline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .faq-item h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .faq-item p {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-variant);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .faq-item a {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* Mission Path Bento style timeline */
  .mission-path {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--spacing-sm);
    background-color: var(--color-surface-dim);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    margin: var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  }

  .path-step {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-outline);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: var(--spacing-md);
    position: relative;
  }

  .step-num {
    position: absolute;
    top: var(--spacing-xs);
    right: var(--spacing-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-primary-container);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  .path-step strong {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    color: var(--color-primary);
    font-size: 1.125rem;
  }

  .path-step p {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--color-on-surface-variant);
    margin-top: 4px;
  }

  .path-arrow {
    display: none;
    justify-content: center;
    color: var(--color-outline-variant);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .mission-path {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: space-between;
    }

    .path-step {
      width: 22%;
      min-height: 120px;
    }

    .path-arrow {
      display: flex;
    }
  }
</style>
